<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>Anima, MOSS and Z-Image — Atelier Guide</title></head><body><header><a class="brand" href="/studio/">← atelier</a><span>Guide · updated October 31, 2026</span></header><main style="max-width:1000px;padding-bottom:70px"><section class="intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">FROM AN IDEA TO A COMPLETE PROMPT</p><h1>Fewer controls. More images.</h1><p>Choose a recipe → describe your subject → generate an image.</p></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Getting started</h2><p>Choose “Guided · fill in a recipe”. The recipe selects the right model. “Cinematic character portrait” is a good starting point. “Use example” fills in a complete scene. Then try changing just the character.</p><p>Choose an aspect ratio such as 1:1 or 16:9, or keep Recommended for the recipe’s original format. Output shows the actual dimensions. Only “What would you like to create?” is required. The service fills in a suitable setting when you leave it blank. Add colors, clothing or important objects in the details field.</p></div><div><h3>A simple starting point</h3><p><strong>Subject:</strong> an adult bounty hunter with short silver hair and a red coat</p><p><strong>Setting:</strong> on a rooftop above a futuristic city at sunset</p><p><strong>Details:</strong> confident expression, wind moving the coat</p><p>The service adds camera framing, lighting, color mood and linework. The full prompt and settings appear directly below the input fields.</p></div><div><h3>What the service prepares</h3><p>Anima: 35 Euler steps, CFG 4.5 and roughly one megapixel in a suitable portrait or landscape format. “Draft” reduces the image size and uses 30 steps; “More detail” uses 45 steps.</p><p>The recipes are written for Aesthetic: no score_* tags, no forced safe/nsfw tags and no hidden translation. You can try other languages; English follows the documented training and prompting examples more reliably.</p></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Choose a direction</h2><p>Character portrait, wide film scene, fantasy, neon night, landscape, cozy slice of life, manga, watercolor and chibi each offer a different composition.</p></div><div><h3>Be specific</h3><p>“an adult mage with braided black hair, green cloak and a wooden staff” says more than “a cool character”. For actions and multiple characters, use clear sentences with positions: left, right, foreground and background.</p><p>Start with one main character and one action. Extreme weights, long lists of quality terms and competing styles can dilute your idea.</p></div><div><h3>When the result misses the mark</h3><p>Reuse the settings of a saved image to keep its seed fixed. Then change only the subject, setting or one detail. A different seed often changes the composition and face more than ten extra steps.</p><p>In free prompt mode, you can choose the sampler, negative prompt and image size yourself. No single combination works for every subject.</p></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Realistic photography with MOSS</h2><p>Choose a recipe under “MOSS · realistic photography”. Only the subject is required. Each recipe supplies a setting, framing, light, materials and a matching image size. “Use example” fills in a complete starting point.</p><p>Recommended uses 50 Euler steps, CFG 3 and Shift 5. Draft uses 30 steps at a smaller size to explore composition; More detail uses 60 steps. Judge skin and material detail at the recommended size.</p></div><div><h3>Pick the right photographic look</h3><p><strong>Natural portrait:</strong> soft daylight, realistic skin and a quiet background. <strong>Candid everyday:</strong> an unposed snapshot with ordinary available light. <strong>Street documentary:</strong> one observed action in a recognizable place. <strong>Cinematic still:</strong> a realistic frame with light motivated by visible lamps or daylight.</p><p><strong>Product studio:</strong> controlled highlights and clear materials. <strong>Landscape and travel:</strong> weather, terrain and atmospheric depth. <strong>Architecture and interior:</strong> room scale, straight verticals and window light. <strong>Food and still life:</strong> visible ingredients, texture and simple styling.</p></div><div><h3>A believable starting point</h3><p><strong>Recipe:</strong> Photo · natural portrait</p><p><strong>Subject:</strong> an adult woman with freckles, short curly brown hair and a dark green linen shirt</p><p><strong>Setting:</strong> sitting beside a large window in a quiet apartment</p><p><strong>Details:</strong> a relaxed expression, visible fine lines, a few loose strands of hair</p><p>Choose concrete visible details instead of stacking “perfect”, “8K” or “masterpiece”. Keep the seed fixed and change one field. Lens references suggest framing and perspective; they do not guarantee physically accurate optics.</p></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Low Poly with MOSS</h2><p>MOSS has dedicated recipes for low-poly characters, karts, props, buildings and geometric 3D scenes. The output is an image, not a 3D model.</p><a href="/studio/lowpoly-guide/">MOSS Low Poly and Blender reference guide ↗</a></div><div><h3>Floating diorama</h3><p><strong>Subject:</strong> a small wooden cabin with a red roof beside a pine tree and a blue pond</p><p><strong>Setting:</strong> on a floating chunk of grassy earth</p><p><strong>Details:</strong> a tiny wooden bridge, angular rocks, warm light in the windows</p></div><div><h3>1990s game scene or game world</h3><p>Try a red rally car on a mountain road or a valley with a turquoise river. The recipe adds angular geometry, visible polygon faces, flat shading, simple materials and a suitable camera angle.</p><p>For quick experiments, start with “Draft”. MOSS uses 50 steps in the recommended profile and takes considerably longer than Anima.</p></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Browse real examples</h2><p>Open Explore in the studio to browse all 72 recipes. Filter by model or search for a look. Every image shows its recorded profile and settings. “Use this example” restores those inputs and its seed; change one field to explore a variation.</p><p>Anima also offers Retro anime, Mecha, Storybook gouache and Dynamic action. MOSS adds Handmade clay, Layered paper cut, Bold comic cover and Macro nature.</p></div><div><h3>Text in the image</h3><p>Z-Image and Z-Image Turbo accept a short visible wording. Start with a short title and inspect every letter. Open an example image to see the full prompt before using it.</p><p>Examples are bundled separately from your saved image library. They do not add jobs or images to your collection. The example quality may be Draft; choose Recommended when you want a larger final image.</p><a href="/studio/#explore">Browse examples ↗</a></div></section><section class="guide"><div><h2>Sources</h2><p><a href="https://huggingface.co/Tongyi-MAI/Z-Image" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official Z-Image model card ↗</a></p></div><div><p><a href="https://huggingface.co/circlestone-labs/Anima" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official Anima model card ↗</a></p><p>Aesthetic-specific tags, sentences, sizes and samplers. The built-in styles are custom recipes based on this guidance.</p></div><div><p><a href="https://huggingface.co/OpenMOSS-Team/MOSS-Image-SFT" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official MOSS SFT model card ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://github.com/OpenMOSS/MOSS-Image/blob/main/USERGUIDE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official MOSS user guide ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://github.com/emrelab/blender-projects" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Low-poly examples by a Blender artist ↗</a></p><p>Anima allows commercial images; paid model hosting requires a separate license. MOSS and Z-Image use Apache 2.0.</p></div></section></main></body></html>
